      "strings": {
        "defendIntro": "Go through the sign-in prompts and password choices one at a time. Deny any prompt you didn't start, and pick the stronger option each time.",
        "teamLine": "By reporting or checking {n} of these rather than going along with them, you kept a risk away from the rest of your team.",
        "weakestTells": "The risks most often missed: {names} ({counts} each).",
        "weakestTell": "The risk most often missed: {names} ({counts}).",
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional haulier, in the depot office. First you're on the red team: look at what a colleague has put online (click a line to note it), build a guess list from it, and watch how fast a reused password opens his work account. Then it's your own account under attack, and you lock it down. Only the lock-down part is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it's your phone.",
        "flipGuessIntro": "You watched a reused password open a colleague's account. Here is the other end: your own phone.",
        "flipIntro": "You watched a reused password open a colleague's account. Here is the other end: your own phone, buzzing with sign-in prompts you didn't start, and a message from 'IT' telling you how to make them stop.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "Someone has a password of yours, from a breach of a site you forgot you used, and they're leaning on your phone because they can't get past it. The number any 'IT' message asks you to read back is on their screen, not IT's, so deny what you didn't start and report it.",
        "flipNoSend": "Nothing of the colleague's is here to replay, but the lesson is the same: a reused password is what opens an account. Next, you lock your own account down.",
        "flipMissed": "The guesser didn't land this time, but a reused password is still what opens an account. Next, you lock your own account down.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing landed this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It opened on a reused password with nothing clever at all. Next, you lock your own account down.",
        "allPhishRight": "Every risk was caught, with every reason named.",
        "noBlameLine": "Reusing a password is ordinary and common; this is how most accounts are broken into, not a sign anyone was careless. A miss here just shows what to change tonight.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday, three things at home: turn on 2-step verification, or a passkey, for your personal email first; check your personal email address on a breach-checking site (the NCSC suggests Have I Been Pwned); and switch on a password manager, starting with your email. Report anything at work using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "Everything here follows one reused password doing the rounds. Anything you were caught out by is shown below, with what to do instead.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "How passwords get stolen",
            "text": "Most passwords aren't cracked by genius. They're guessed from the obvious first (123456, a team, a pet, the company name and a year), reused from a breach of another site, phished through a fake sign-in page, or simply handed over. A different password for every account, kept in a manager, is the one habit that blunts all of those at once."
          },
          {
            "heading": "The sign-in ladder",
            "text": "Turn on a second step beyond the password wherever you can. A passkey is best: it only works on the real site, so it can't be phished or relayed. An authenticator app with number matching is next. A text code is a last resort, but still far better than a password alone."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Browsers, notes and managers",
            "text": "A password manager makes and remembers a different password for every account and fills it in only on the right site. Your browser's own manager is fine for personal accounts on your own devices; at work, use what the organisation provides. Never leave a password on a note on a shared desk, monitor or keyboard."
          },
          {
            "heading": "What a passkey is",
            "text": "A passkey replaces the password with a secret kept on your device or in your manager, unlocked the way you unlock your phone. Because it is tied to the real website, it can't be typed into a fake page, reused on another site, or leaked in someone else's breach."
          }
        ]
      },
